---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Header } from '../components/layout/Header';
import Footer from '../components/layout/Footer.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy — SecurePass"
  description="SecurePass privacy: passwords are generated locally and never stored, synced, or transmitted."
  canonicalPath="/privacy"
>
  <Header client:load />
  <main class="sp-container max-w-3xl py-10">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">Privacy</h1>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted sm:text-base">
      <p class="rounded-lg border border-success/30 bg-success/10 px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">
        Your passwords are generated locally in your browser. They are not sent to our servers.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing. SecurePass has no accounts, no analytics, no tracking pixels, and no
        third-party scripts that can observe generated passwords. There is no server
        component that could receive your data in the first place.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">What is stored on your device</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Theme preference only.</strong> If you pick light, dark, or system theme,
          that single preference is saved in your browser’s local storage so the choice
          persists. Nothing else is written there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Generated passwords are never stored.</strong> Not in local storage, session
          storage, cookies, the page URL, or browser history. The optional history feature
          keeps entries in the page’s memory only and wipes them when you reload or close
          the tab.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Network activity</h2>
      <p>
        After the page loads, SecurePass makes no network requests of its own. Copying a
        password uses your operating system’s clipboard; nothing about the password is
        transmitted.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Hosting</h2>
      <p>
        This is a static site — plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files. The hosting provider
        necessarily sees standard web-server logs (like any website), but the application
        itself sends it nothing about your passwords.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
